  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A cold front moves through on Thursday Night, \nwith scattered showers and isolated thunderstorms possible ahead\nof the front, mainly for the Lehigh Valley, Poconos, and \nnorthern New Jersey Thursday afternoon and evening. \n\nHigh pressure moves offshore today but will remain in control\nresulting in a beautiful Wednesday with highs in the upper\n70s/low 80s. \n\nThe high moves further offshore tonight into Thursday with a\ncold front moving into the northwest. Forcing is not very strong\nthough with the front and instability/shear will be lacking, so\nnot much in terms of impacts expected with this frontal passage.\nHowever, some scattered showers and isolated thunderstorms \ncould develop out ahead of the front on Thursday. Coverage of\nany precipitation will be mainly over the Lehigh Valley,\nPoconos, and northern New Jersey during the day on Thursday. As\nthe front pushes through on Thursday evening and Thursday Night,\nsome scattered showers will push through the rest of the area,\nbut nothing of concern is expected and rainfall amounts will be\nunder a tenth of an inch. High pressure then moves in for Friday\nand Saturday, with a two pack of relatively quiet days expected.\n\nA stretch of above normal temperatures will continue for today\nand peak on Thursday with highs in the low to mid 80s. The\npassing front on Thursday Night will knock down temperatures a\nbit on Friday, but highs should still be above normal. The\ncooler airmass lags behind the front and won't arrive until\nFriday Night/Saturday, where more seasonable temperatures are\nexpected. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A stretch of unsettled weather begins on \nSunday, continuing into the first half of next week. Monitoring \nthe potential for heavy rain. \n\nA warm front lifts through on Saturday Night, ushering in a more\nhumid airmass. A cold front approaches from the west on Sunday,\nwith showers and thunderstorms expected to develop out ahead of\nthe front. Sunday looks pretty active and unsettled as the front\nslowly pushes in. The current forecast has 50-80% PoPs across\nthe region for the daytime period. \n\nThe front is expected to stall over the region for the first\nhalf of the week, with multiple waves of low pressure moving\nalong the boundary. This will result in periods of rain with\nperhaps a few thunderstorms mixed in each day. There is some\nconcern for heavy rain and flash flooding as a humid airmass\nshould be in place with a slow moving or stalled boundary\noverhead. However, we are now in meteorological fall, and the\nquestion remains how much instability will be in place which\nwill factor into rainfall rates. NBM Probability of 1 inch or\ngreater Sunday and Monday is around 35-55%. Still in the day 5\nto 7 range with this stretch, so definitely some uncertainty in\nthe forecast and things could change. \n\nThe unsettled pattern should continue through the middle of the\nweek until a sweeping cold front comes through. Most medium\nrange guidance has that front coming through sometime Wednesday\nor Thursday.",
